\f1\fs22 Sarah\'92s reverie snapped when she saw Chanta\'92s jaw harden and his eyes go steel. Suddenly, sweet Chanta was shouldering past her, muscling the rest of the row back into their chairs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He was off, all intent and menace. Sarah scrambled after him, as, behind her, the Elvi who had just given the divine performance started bickering over lyrics. A punch-up broke out on stage. Buskers charged up with guitars and amps, everyone desperate to be first.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She nearly missed the Sages. At first, she didn\'92t recognise them: three string-bean wise men with Gandalf beards\'97Kleo in a jumpsuit of many colours and flowing cape, Solo strapped into leather from collar to ankle, Chilo sweating through army surplus. Chilo stepped up to block Chanta, Solo wisely retreated, and Kleo simply waited, serene as ever.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta rammed Chilo in the chest, sending him sprawling over a lemonade stand. Then Chanta snatched Kleo by the jumpsuit collar and, finding him light as wicker, tossed him at the cowering Solo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tony!\'94 Sarah bolted past and knelt by the Sages.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo rose, dusted off, and nodded. \'93Ah, your qualities are as advertised, young Anthony.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta jabbed a finger, bristling. \'93Stay away from Sarah or I\'92ll\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, yes,\'94 Kleo interrupted with infinite patience. \'93Enough. I\'92ve had my fill of boyish outbursts tonight.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta advanced.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah slid between them. \'93Tony, can I\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The prophecy demands a child be born,\'94 Chilo interjected.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s jaw dropped. \'93A what now?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo closed his eyes, breathing out slow embarrassment before nodding. \'93Recent events suggest the prophecy approaches fruition.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah groaned. \'93Oh. So you mean the \'91recent events\'92 you\'92ve been meddling with and nudging all along?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Surely you see, this is facilitative oversight. It\'92s our job,\'94 Kleo replied, as if citing a legal brief.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sounds like the world\'92s most elaborate pick-up attempt,\'94 Sarah snapped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Hardly elaborate,\'94 Kleo countered. \'93We strive for minimal, systemically sensitive interventions.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I bet all three of you have very small interventions.\'94 Chanta\'92s bent-pinky-finger gesture said it all.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah cracked sarcastic: \'93So whoever draws the shortest\'85 intervention\'85 gets to fuck me? Or do I get the full three-wizard gang bang?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No one\'92s fucking you!\'94 Chanta objected. \'93Unless\'85 you really want\'85\'94 He trailed off.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There\'92s too much at stake,\'94 Solo added, for gravitas. \'93We beg you, think bigger.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta hitched up his pants. \'93Oh, so now they\'92re talking about me,\'94 he said with a ridiculous grin.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We must act now,\'94 Chilo insisted. \'93A child, by Christmas.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93In the name of tax, why Christmas?\'94 Sarah demanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We like eggnog,\'94 Solo grinned, always the one to rescue tragic scenes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah rolled her eyes, sarcasm returning in force. \'93And after that? Where is this prophecy even going?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93To the end of the universe!\'94 Chilo blurted. Kleo\'92s long breath barely contained his horror at the answer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What?\'94 Sarah and Chanta shouted in unison.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just admit it,\'94 Solo wheedled, \'93this universe is completely taxed.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo elbowed Solo. \'93So a new, perfect universe can bang into place,\'94 he said, earning a stomp from Kleo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah laughed. \'93How will you know if you succeed? We\'92ll all be dead.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo peered down his nose, silent.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Okay,\'94 Sarah said, encouraging them to play it out just so she could vent at them. \'93So I\'92m meant to have a baby and end the universe. Great! Who does my kid become, exactly\'97the Antichrist? Hitler?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92d like to name him Richard,\'94 Chilo ventured.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You want me to give birth to Dick Hitler.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo considered Sarah\'92s sarcasm, and nodded with just a hint of bureaucracy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You could call him Little Richard, Sarah,\'94 Chanta offered, finally claiming the fool\'92s crown.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The words \'93Little Richard\'94 floated out and, in that strange way of the Ghetto, travelled faster than the speed of sound. Pompadours quivered. Collars stiffened. A few sunglasses fogged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A clutch of Elvi materialised around them, postures mixing reverence and wary disapproval.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not this again,\'94 one Elvis half-whispered, then, to Sarah: \'93Not to tell you your business, but there are names best left for legends. And none of us wants a repeat of the Tutti Frutti Summit of \'9269.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>An older Elvis shook his head, jumpsuit zippers tensed. \'93Graceland\'92s got room for all kinds, but give something the wrong label and folks get tetchy fast. It was always a friendly rivalry\'97friendly\'92s the key.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo, quick to mediate, raised his hands. \'93Perhaps we could call the prospective child\'85 Clinton? Clinton\'92s his given first name, after all.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At once, the Elvi relaxed. But no one but the Sages knew who Kleo was talking about.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93
\f2\i The
\f1\i0  Dawkins?\'94 Chilo prompted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The One,\'94 Solo explained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After an awkward pause, the Elvi echoed \'93Little Clinton,\'94 playing with the name like a lyric, and working it into melodies. \'93Now there\'92s a handle you can dance to.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Immediately, arguments began:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s \'91Little Clinton, don\'92t you hit me once or twice!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Hang on, \'91Little Blister, don\'92t you Clinton me\'92\'97I\'92m sure that\'92s what I sang in \'9262, arm in a sling, after the pie fight.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nah, you\'92re all twisted. Everyone knows it\'92s Lawdy Miss Clinty. It was all up and down the club charts winter of \'9258.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Lawdy Miss Clinty? Wasn\'92t that banned for medical reasons?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Only in Tennessee. In the Ghetto, it hits number one every year someone tries to spell it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Honestly, it\'92s \'91Good Luck Clinton\'92. My nan had the \'9245. Lost it in a bet with a man dressed as a pineapple.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And then there\'92s the acetate from Tupelo. Elvis slurred it so bad, but you could still hear \'91Little Clinton in a Cadillac\'92, clear as day.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The scrum of capes and high jaws built. Sarah edged back, wondering if saying anything else would set off a whole new musical schism.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eventually, Sarah lost patience. \'93There will be no baby,\'94 she shouted above the bickering. \'93Close the songbook.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta couldn\'92t resist: \'93What about a cat, then?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>An Elvis glared: \'93Okay, but you can\'92t call it Little Richard.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Through all of this, Kleo\'92s posture wilted. \'93Chilo, see where you\'92ve led us? I need a drink.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A hush. Every Elvi face tracked Sarah, wary.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Allow me,\'94 Solo said, dashing off.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi dispersed while the remaining four waited in strained silence\'97Sarah watching for the next stunt, Chanta hoping his stock was rising. Solo returned with pints of peanut-scented cocktails and handed them around.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Be a good chap,\'94 Chilo muttered to Solo, \'93and distract the marks.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What?\'94 Solo mumbled, raising his pint and swallowing a chunk of pineapple whole. He gagged, spraying pink froth; Sarah and Chanta recoiled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well done,\'94 Chilo said, quietly dropping potions into their drinks.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo emitted a sigh of last-ditch disappointment. \'93So it\'92s come to this, then.\'94\

